1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,180
Speaker 1: Hey y'all, Peter Zine here coming to you from Colorado, hiking season.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,560
Speaker 1: Unfortunately, I can still get the news, so I know that we now have a 50% tariff

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:14,760
Speaker 1: courtesy of Donald Trump that is going to be coming to U.S. copper imports beginning August 1.

4
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,760
Speaker 1: Let's say that you are someone who is really concerned about the U.S. copper industry.

5
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:22,900
Speaker 1: I am not, I'm really not.

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:29,340
Speaker 1: But if you are, there are a few things that you do before you get larger volumes of copper

7
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:30,480
Speaker 1: from domestic production.

8
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,900
Speaker 1: Number one, you go out and explore to find the deposit you're after.

9
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:39,760
Speaker 1: Number two, you build physical infrastructure, road and rail, to get to that system that can

10
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:40,500
Speaker 1: handle heavy freight.

11
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:46,160
Speaker 1: Third, you build a processing facility to separate the copper ore from the rest of the rock, and

12
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:49,600
Speaker 1: then an intermediate processing facility to turn it into something called blister copper,

13
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,180
Speaker 1: which is roughly 98% pure, gets rid of a lot of the sulfur.

14
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:58,440
Speaker 1: You then take that blister copper and you take it to another facility that you need to build

15
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:03,940
Speaker 1: that's a proper smelter that will turn it into like, you know, the reddish orange, shiny stuff

16
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:04,880
Speaker 1: that you use in everything.

17
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,680
Speaker 1: And then copper then goes on to be in almost everything that involves electricity.

18
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,160
Speaker 1: So it is an important material.

19
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,560
Speaker 1: But putting a 50% tariff on it on the front end retards that entire process.

20
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:22,600
Speaker 1: And from start to finish, that entire process takes somewhere between 10 and 15 years.

21
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:29,960
Speaker 1: So if your goal was to facilitate copper production, step one would be offer, say, tax credits for

22
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:30,540
Speaker 1: exploration.

23
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:36,540
Speaker 1: Go ahead and build the physical infrastructure and get started on the smelters.

24
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:41,040
Speaker 1: All of that is very power intensive, so you all sort of need some more electricity.

25
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:45,100
Speaker 1: By putting the tariff on the front end, you're basically retarding the whole process rather

26
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:45,840
Speaker 1: than speeding it.

27
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:48,160
Speaker 1: That's problem one.

28
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,080
Speaker 1: Problem two is that the Chinese are literally dying out.

29
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:56,680
Speaker 1: And while they are big players in the copper sector, and that will have to be shook out at

30
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:58,900
Speaker 1: some point, that's not my primary concern at this point.

31
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,820
Speaker 1: My primary concern is we have a limited amount of time in the United States to build out our

32
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:07,220
Speaker 1: industrial plant to prepare for the Chinese just not being there.

33
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:10,740
Speaker 1: And that means roughly doubling the size of the industrial plant.

34
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:16,720
Speaker 1: And for that first stage, doubling the size, there are four main inputs that you need.

35
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,680
Speaker 1: The first one is copper.

36
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,260
Speaker 1: That now costs 50% more than it used to.

37
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:23,860
Speaker 1: Or it will on August 1.

38
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:30,680
Speaker 1: The second item is steel, primarily but not exclusively for structure and interior structure support.

39
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:31,340
Speaker 1: I think I-beams.

40
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:36,600
Speaker 1: That, courtesy of an existing Trump tariff, is now 50% more than it used to be.

41
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,260
Speaker 1: Third is aluminum, primarily but not exclusively, used in cladding and especially HVAC systems.

42
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,640
Speaker 1: That is now 50% more than it used to be because of Trump tariffs.

43
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:53,640
Speaker 1: And the fourth thing you need is a labor force that's willing to do the construction work.

44
00:02:54,460 --> 00:03:02,960
Speaker 1: Now, in the United States, historically, for the last 40 years, most of that work has been done by immigrants from Mexico and Central America.

45
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:09,420
Speaker 1: But as you may have noticed, the Trump administration has basically launched a pogrom against illegal migrants.

46
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:17,860
Speaker 1: Now, I don't want to get into a broad debate of the pros and cons of immigration at this moment, but let's just talk about where this policy in its current form leads.

47
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:27,740
Speaker 1: The Trump administration wants to deport about a million people a year, which carried out for a few years would basically remove the illegal migrant community in its entirety.

48
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,400
Speaker 1: Construction is the industry that they are most involved in.

49
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:32,580
Speaker 1: Agriculture is number two.

50
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:46,040
Speaker 1: And what the Trump administration has discovered is that going after people who have committed crimes is actually kind of hard because it's a law enforcement issue and you have to do investigations and arrest them one at a time.

51
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,780
Speaker 1: That's not going to get you to a million people.

52
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:52,680
Speaker 1: So instead, they're going after people that they know about.

53
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:53,720
Speaker 1: They're going to churches.

54
00:03:54,500 --> 00:04:01,340
Speaker 1: They're revoking legal status for people who, say, have been brought in from Venezuela or Haiti out of economic or political persecution.

55
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:08,720
Speaker 1: They're going to people's court hearings where they're going to get ruled on for, say, a green card and arresting them before they can be for the judge.

56
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:11,840
Speaker 1: Because, you know, these are people where they know where they are.

57
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:21,800
Speaker 1: So the four inputs that we need to prepare for a post-China world are now more expensive.

58
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:25,600
Speaker 1: And every time the cost of something goes up, you can do less of it.

59
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:42,320
Speaker 1: So if these policies continue for any appreciable amount of time, we can kiss that economic boom that I've been talking about for years goodbye, because we will not have the industrial plant that is necessary to produce the goods we need to continue to lead the lives that we have been leading.

60
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:52,400
Speaker 1: It's almost as if a Russian agent was whispering things in Trump's ear and trying to convince him to do the things that would be most against our best interests.

61
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:55,340
Speaker 2: Oh, wait.
